    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e${{cost_ps}} a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost) for a high-protein dinner that beats takeout on price and macros.",
    "shop_smart":  ["\u003cstrong\u003eCrushed tomatoes in the large can\u003c/strong\u003e are one of the cheapest ways to build a big pot of sauce, and this recipe leans right into that.","A pound of \u003cstrong\u003ewhole carrots\u003c/strong\u003e costs less than pre-shredded and shreds in seconds. Small savings that add up across a batch.","Dried \u003cstrong\u003eitalian seasoning from a single jar\u003c/strong\u003e covers this and a dozen other recipes, so buy it once and forget about it.","\u003cstrong\u003eBuy the protein penne, not the regular blue box.\u003c/strong\u003e It comes in a 14.5 ounce box instead of the usual pound, so count your boxes off 14.5 and you will not come up short. It costs more than plain pasta, and the protein on this card is exactly what you are paying for."],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Heat the olive oil in a large pot and brown 5.4 pounds of 93/7 ground beef with the chopped onion, shredded carrots, and finely chopped celery, seasoning with the salt, black pepper, and a pinch of sugar, then drain.",
                    "Stir in the crushed tomatoes, tomato paste, beef broth, worcestershire sauce, garlic, and italian seasoning.",
                    "Boil the penne separately until just tender, then drain.",
                    "Simmer the sauce a few minutes so it thickens, then fold in the cooked pasta.",
                    "Stir in the parmesan and portion it into containers."
